   1:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c **** /**
   2:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  * @file
   3:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  * Address Resolution Protocol module for IP over Ethernet
   4:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  *
   5:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  * Functionally, ARP is divided into two parts. The first maps an IP address
   6:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  * to a physical address when sending a packet, and the second part answers
   7:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  * requests from other machines for our physical address.
   8:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  *
   9:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  * This implementation complies with RFC 826 (Ethernet ARP). It supports
  10:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  * Gratuitious ARP from RFC3220 (IP Mobility Support for IPv4) section 4.6
  11:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  * if an interface calls etharp_gratuitous(our_netif) upon address change.
  12:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/ipv4/etharp.c ****  */
